# Covenant Engine & Timezone Revision 2 Audit

## Request implemented

This revision applies the latest Universal how-to-play system and makes province- and settlement-level timezones mechanically relevant to random encounters.

## Rules migration

The previous add-on still generated encounters primarily from fifth-edition adjusted-XP thresholds and ordinary descending individual initiative. Revision 2 replaces those runtime assumptions with the current Covenant Engine procedures:

- Light, Standard, Hard, and Extreme Threat Point budgets at 3/4/5/6 TP per player character;
- enemy ratings of 1 TP minion, 2 TP minor, 4 TP peer, 7 TP elite, and 10–16 TP boss;
- objective and severe-terrain additions, plus allied-support and decisive-preparation reductions;
- alternating-side initiative after the highest initiative result determines the first side;
- three Beats and one Reaction per turn;
- 2-Beat converted Multiattack;
- extra boss initiative slots;
- a non-HP objective, Progress clock, Danger clock, several valid approaches, and open resolution.

Adjusted XP is retained only for stat-block compatibility and export visibility. It does not set the encounter budget.

## Time and calendar implementation

The new `universal-time.js` runtime provides:

- Earth UTC as the permanent world zero reference;
- no daylight saving time anywhere;
- settlement-over-province timezone precedence;
- explicit fixed offset and longitude-derived fallback support;
- minute, fractional-hour, and whole-hour offsets from UTC−12:00 through UTC+12:00;
- world UTC and local date rollover;
- 11 months of 30 days;
- an intercalary day after Month 11 every seventh year;
- duration conversion between 330.15 Universal astronomical days and 365.2422 Earth days;
- the supplied named meridians and parallels;
- local dayparts and schedule tags;
- timezone-crossing schedule shifts that do not alter elapsed duration.

## Encounter effects

The encounter record now stores local and UTC time together. Local daypart affects candidate weighting and creates encounter context for:

- markets, work shifts, deliveries, public services, and crowds;
- gates, watches, patrol changes, rites, and institutional schedules;
- travel departures and return traffic;
- witnesses, visibility, and access;
- smuggling, covert movement, nightlife, night patrols, and nocturnal activity.

The add-on never derives activity schedule from ancestry, deity, class, or moral alignment.

## Player autonomy

Every generated scene explicitly states that objectives, clocks, faction pressure, covenant pressure, and hooks create consequences and urgency but do not choose a player character’s action. Six approach categories are supplied, including an open player-created approach.

## Compatibility retained

- 326 hostile records
- exactly 22 deities and covenants
- exactly 22 merged deity-bound classes and 66 subclasses
- exactly 236 canonical race entries
- all eight alignment axes and 81 profiles
- level-8 minimum and level-30 add-on support
- multiclass total-level preservation
- class-to-deity enforcement
- ancestry-independent morality
- Ichor equipment, reactions, attack memory, dialogue, tokens, grids, and ActiveWorkspace damage hooks
- full offline fallback

## Test result

All final tests passed. The live loader generated 1,000 seeded encounters and the offline fallback generated 250. Tests covered all seven local dayparts, settlement overrides, province fallback, longitude fallback, fractional and minute offsets, named coordinate lines, ordinary date rollover, leap-day rollover, Threat Point overage limits, objective clocks, alternating initiative, boss slots, and three-Beat hostile plans.
